{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"SETTLED — with credentials in the RIGHT account (896586841071 / us-east-1, user joe_frontier), the IAM question I previously could not answer is now definitive, and the finding is CONFIRMED. This plan's grant is wrong as written.\n\nEVIDENCE (aws iam simulate-custom-policy, against a policy granting ONLY what this plan proposes):\n\n  policy = Allow bedrock:InvokeModel + bedrock:InvokeModelWithResponseStream on *\n    bedrock:InvokeModel    -> allowed\n    bedrock:Converse       -> implicitDeny\n    bedrock:ConverseStream -> implicitDeny\n\nbedrock:Converse is a DISTINCT action namespace from bedrock:InvokeModel. rebar reaches Bedrock through pydantic-ai's BedrockProvider, which calls the Converse API (confirmed in-repo by the measured error text in tests/unit/test_bedrock_provider.py: \"when calling the Converse operation\"). So the grant as planned yields AccessDeniedException at runtime, this story's own AC (a minimal bedrock-runtime Converse call from the instance) cannot pass, and story eb6e would cut the production review bot over to Bedrock with NO Anthropic fallback on a role that cannot invoke it.\n\nALSO CONFIRMED: simulate-principal-policy against the real arn:aws:iam::896586841071:role/rebar-gerrit-instance-role returns implicitDeny for all four of InvokeModel / InvokeModelWithResponseStream / Converse / ConverseStream — i.e. the role has NO Bedrock access today, so this grant is genuinely additive rather than a widening of something existing.\n\nREQUIRED FIX to this plan: grant bedrock:Converse and bedrock:ConverseStream (alongside, or instead of, the InvokeModel pair — keep InvokeModel only if some other code path actually uses it; rebar's does not). Then re-run the simulation against the role and require \"allowed\" for Converse before landing.\n\nWHY THIS WAS INVISIBLE EARLIER, worth recording so the trap is not re-set: my previous credential held AmazonBedrockFullAccess (bedrock:*) in a DIFFERENT account. Every Converse call succeeded regardless of which action it required, so success proved nothing. Never validate a scoped policy with a wildcard-holding identity — and check the ACCOUNT, not just that a call worked."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785427467121354001,"uuid":"37c7b648-c98c-4cc2-88fb-fd1f7f12ec38"}